#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167858} :activity/id #uuid "ffe540ef-f07a-55b2-bc12-0253ba59e610" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048992 :topic/id #uuid "d1b1f9a6-d57b-5e8a-a131-1da2ca6d5c52" :topic/math-academy-id 3239} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167853 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168000 :tutorial/content "Sketch the phase portrait of the system of linear differential equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t)=-2x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)=2x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ngiven that the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=-4$ and $\\lambda_2=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ respectively.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny =-\\dfrac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{-4t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/14/1459d79913dec89577405a506b8d4adbbf4cb05abb51948eedae76d8f71544b8.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny= x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince $e^{2t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/fd/fd53399b3d0c5ec40adcf91aaae4aca5ce55a9a7b574e9223fde615afc3b769f.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/bf/bf0c26af74326dd108d42eec1493b0ddf64cfdf0744c91b55e732b80b7d0835c.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "031f896a-f5ca-51ae-a8e9-aa3cbdeb5224" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14454 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example: One Positive and One Negative Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "0c9b5bdd-cfb3-5831-a6f1-a3cc4b3ee417" :step/math-academy-id 588231 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167854}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167854 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168007 :tutorial/content "Let's sketch the phase portrait of the system of linear differential equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)=2x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ngiven that the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ respectively.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny= 2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{5t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c7/c7c824e97d40d2f982525040d5f0ba697c34c40ff51700ec3952d2909d566e21.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{2t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c3/c3030613b03a1afabd964d9494bf2359bd6ce5f021c731feda9607a81aaf5df6.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n\n$$\n0 < \\lambda_2 < \\lambda_1\n$$\n\nthus, $\\lambda_1 = 5$ is the dominant eigenvalue.\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is $\\lambda_1=5,$ solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/43/43c6294fa7f68b21422eee71d35edd5f7880b3b3c9e23a2a8078090416cded05.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "399c5598-6334-5eba-a0dc-06d8a62a0dfc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14469 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example: Two Positive Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "f8b852c5-452c-526f-ba30-6b04d1f42cf7" :step/math-academy-id 588232 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167855}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166916 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166541 :question/id #uuid "9377994d-f08b-57ee-8bd5-2315bf74a70f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21632"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ada3a84a-f979-5aee-9422-7acb8e430c11" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048452}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308396}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Phase Portrait of a System"} :step/id #uuid "69213cd0-fe18-534a-aea0-2c593cf69f25" :step/math-academy-id 588233 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167856}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167856 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166917 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166544 :question/id #uuid "986f9190-9e73-5e08-b08d-ec779933b0cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-21635"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13af6d27-628d-5bdb-b396-299d9cfd64e7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308459}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Parameters of a System Given Its Phase Portrait"} :step/id #uuid "949cba7d-2d28-59d9-862e-307c271e6fe7" :step/math-academy-id 588236 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167859}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167857 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166918 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166543 :question/id #uuid "1c7567cf-f861-5088-b43e-ff94fcff0c91" :question/math-academy-id "e-21634"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35492718-d2d7-5c9d-9c02-1ef6ae0445d0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048990}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391226}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "122db1c2-fd77-51a9-b4db-994bd666caf3" :step/math-academy-id 588239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167858 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168021 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we'll study *phase portraits* of linear systems\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nin the case where the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ has two *real, distinct* eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ with corresponding eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2.$\n\nIf $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c\\,e^{\\lambda t}\\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\ngives us a solution that represents a line through the origin parallel to $\\mathbf{v}.$ So, when $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ are real and distinct, we always have two special straight-line solutions:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1 e^{\\lambda_1 t}\\mathbf{v}_1\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_2 e^{\\lambda_2 t}\\mathbf{v}_2\n$$\n\nAlong the line parallel to the eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}$:\n\n- If $\\lambda<0,$ then $e^{\\lambda t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to\\infty,$ so trajectories move *toward* the origin (*stable line*).\n\n- If $\\lambda>0,$ then $e^{\\lambda t}\\to\\infty$ as $t\\to\\infty,$ so trajectories move *away* from the origin (*unstable line*).\n\nUsually, to sketch the phase portrait, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Draw the two eigenvector lines through the origin (one line parallel to the eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and another parallel to the eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_2$).\n\n- Add arrows on each eigenvector line:\n\n- toward the origin if its eigenvalue is negative, or\n\n- away from the origin if its eigenvalue is positive.\n\n- Add a few curved trajectories that are generally \"pulled\" toward the stable direction(s) and \"pushed\" along unstable direction(s).\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "fc9385f4-31a9-5843-ac73-7335f2530b76" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14566 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "37b016bb-681e-53fd-be32-a1388eb7951d" :step/math-academy-id 588257 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167853}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167859 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168022 :tutorial/content "Finally, let’s summarize what we’ve learned so far.\n\nAgain, we are considering a linear system\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nin the case where the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ has two *real, distinct* eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ with corresponding eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2.$\n\nRecall that if $\\lambda_1, \\lambda_2 \\neq 0,$ the system has a single equilibrium point at the origin $(0,0).$\nTo classify this equilibrium, we look at the signs of $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2.$\n\nFirst, we describe the straight-line solutions:\n\n- Along the line parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_1,$ solutions have the form $\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1e^{\\lambda_1 t}\\mathbf{v}_1$ where $c_1 \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- If $\\lambda_1<0,$ it's a *stable* line (direction).\n\n- If $\\lambda_1>0,$ it's an *unstable* line (direction).\n\n- Along the line parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_2,$ solutions have the form $\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_2e^{\\lambda_2 t}\\mathbf{v}_2$ where $c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- If $\\lambda_2<0,$ it's a *stable* line (direction).\n\n- If $\\lambda_2>0,$ it's an *unstable* line (direction).\n\nAs for the classification of the equilibrium at the origin, we get the following:\n\n- *Unstable node (source)* if $\\lambda_1>0$ and $\\lambda_2>0.$\n\n- *Stable node (sink)* if $\\lambda_1<0$ and $\\lambda_2<0.$ In fact, it's asymptotically stable, as the trajectories converge to the origin.\n\n- *Saddle point* if $\\lambda_1\\lambda_2<0$ (one positive, one negative).\n\nLet's apply this classification to concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "93564ac3-0171-5011-adda-f2d7ed859149" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14567 :tutorial/title "Classification of Equilibria"} :step/id #uuid "6e0c73bf-377f-5bc2-934d-f43778a0de4b" :step/math-academy-id 588258 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167857}}] :activity/title "Phase Portraits for Linear Systems With Real Distinct Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167010}]}
